{"about":"Atom viewer data for all 118 elements. Built by web/atom/build_atoms.py from saved RSC pages. No em dashes.","sources":{"rsc":{"cite":"Royal Society of Chemistry, Periodic Table, one page per element (periodic-table.rsc.org).","url":"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/","used_for":"Ground-state electron configuration (fact box) and the isotope table, from which the most abundant natural isotope sets the neutron count. Noble-gas cores are expanded with RSC's own configuration for that noble gas.","read":"2026-10-01"},"openstax-6-3":{"cite":"OpenStax, Chemistry 2e, section 6.3 Development of Quantum Theory (Figure 6.21, shapes of s, p, d and f orbitals; orbitals per subshell 2l + 1).","url":"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory","used_for":"Orbital shapes shown in orbital mode and the 2l + 1 orbitals per subshell.","read":"2026-10-01"},"openstax-6-4":{"cite":"OpenStax, Chemistry 2e, section 6.4 Electronic Structure of Atoms (Electron Configurations).","url":"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations","used_for":"Subshell capacities (s 2, p 6, d 10, f 14), Hund's rule (one electron per orbital before pairing), noble-gas core notation.","read":"2026-10-01"},"pubchem-pt":{"cite":"National Library of Medicine, PubChem Periodic Table of Elements, electron configuration column.","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/periodic-table/","used_for":"The predicted tag: PubChem labels the configuration of element 109 (calculated) and 110 to 118 (predicted), so elements from 109 on carry a Predicted tag. The configurations themselves still come from RSC.","read":"2026-10-01"},"method":"Cloud shapes are drawn from the textbook hydrogen-atom orbitals (radial part times the real angular part for each n, l, m), resized per shell so they nest. A teaching picture, not a computed many-electron wavefunction."},"ui":{"_note":"Visitor-facing words for the atom viewer. Placeholders